Location: src/garden/invite.rs:47-53
Context: invite peer values are checked for characters only, not for shape or destination
```
fn is_plain_peer(address: &str) -> bool {
    !address.is_empty()
        && address.len() <= MAX_PEER_BYTES
        && address
            .chars()
            .all(|c| c.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || matches!(c, '/' | '.' | ':' | '-' | '_'))
}
```

Location: src/garden/node.rs:540-546
Context: bootstrap dials every invite peer before the genesis or the token is checked
```
    let mut dialed = 0usize;
    for peer in &invite.peers {
        if let Ok(address) = peer.parse() {
            let _ = garden_net.commands.send(NetCommand::Dial(address)).await;
            dialed += 1;
        }
    }
```

How to fix:
Require announced and invite addresses to be exactly one `ip4|ip6|dns4|dns6` + `tcp/<port>` + `p2p/<key>` triple with the peer id last, so the socket that is dialed is the one the identity check covers, and stop treating the presence of a peer id anywhere in the address as evidence about the destination. Then decide, explicitly, what a Garden may connect to: an outbound destination policy (refusing loopback, link-local and private ranges, or an operator-configured allowlist) is what actually removes the ability to aim a node at an internal service, and it needs to be applied to both the validator table and the invite seed list. Bound the capability as well: back off after a failed handshake so a member cannot re-aim peers at one target on every round, and correct the `validator_dial_address` comment so the next reader is not told an invariant that the code does not hold.

Please fix this vulnerability. If you propose a fix, make it concise and minimal.
`````
