### 🟡 Garden evicts a deposit from every queue but leaves the file on disk when a pending Publish names it, so a member parks unaccounted content on every peer

**Severity:** MEDIUM · Deep review

**TL;DR:** `note_unreferenced` in `src/garden/node.rs` pops an evicted deposit out of all queues before deciding whether to delete it; when a pending `Publish` names the id the delete is skipped, so the file stays on disk with nothing accounting for it.

**Impact:** A Garden member can push multi-MiB objects into every other member's node outside the per-validator content share and the deposit budget, and nothing frees them while the Garden runs, so peers' disks fill up with content nobody is charged for.

**Root cause:**
- The per-peer deposit queue is the only record of an object no publication accounts for, yet eviction removes the id from that queue first and decides about deletion afterwards, so a protected deposit escapes the accounting instead of staying queued or being refused.
- `is_pending_content` shields any content id named by any `Publish` transaction in the mempool, and `sender_may_pool` admits `Publish` from any key in the validator table, including a key that has left or been suspended, so such a transaction can never apply and never stop naming.
- `GardenContentStore` keeps no index and `open` adopts an existing directory unchanged, so once an id is out of the queues the only paths that can ever remove the file are the same eviction and the wipe on leaving.

